{"metadata": {"file_suffix": "pdf", "producer": {"name": "mineru", "version": "4.0.10"}, "document": {"page_count": 2145, "page_count_kind": "physical"}}, "extensions": {"mineru": {"tier": "standard", "parse_mode": "txt"}, "docvortex_layout": {"version": 1, "pages": [{"page_idx": 124, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 125, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 126, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 127, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 128, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 129, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 130, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 131, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 132, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 133, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 134, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 135, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 136, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 137, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 138, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 139, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 140, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 141, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}]}}, "pages": [{"page_idx": 124, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.139, 0.079, 0.855, 0.264], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Updating this internal state information as time goes by requires two kinds of knowledge to be encoded in the agent program in some form. First, we need some information about how the world changes over time, which can be divided roughly into two parts: the effects of the agent’s actions and how the world evolves independently of the agent. For example, when the agent turns the steering wheel clockwise, the car turns to the right, and when it’s raining the car’s cameras can get wet. This knowledge about “how the world works”—whether implemented in simple Boolean circuits or in complete scientific theories—is called a transition model of the world.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.218, 0.329, 0.348, 0.346], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Transition model", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.139, 0.412, 0.85, 0.526], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Second, we need some information about how the state of the world is reflected in the agent’s percepts. For example, when the car in front initiates braking, one or more illuminated red regions appear in the forward-facing camera image, and, when the camera gets wet, droplet-shaped objects appear in the image partially obscuring the road. This kind of knowledge is called a sensor model.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.218, 0.592, 0.319, 0.609], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Sensor model", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.14, 0.675, 0.854, 0.741], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Together, the transition model and sensor model allow an agent to keep track of the state of the world—to the extent possible given the limitations of the agent’s sensors. An agent that uses such models is called a model-based agent.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.217, 0.807, 0.361, 0.825], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Model-based agent", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 125, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.139, 0.078, 0.823, 0.241], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Figure 2.11 gives the structure of the model-based reflex agent with internal state,", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "showing how the current percept is combined with the old internal state to generate the updated description of the current state, based on the agent’s model of how the world works. The agent program is shown in Figure 2.12 . The interesting part is the function", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "UPDATE-STATE, which is responsible for creating the new internal state description. The details of how models and states are represented vary widely depending on the type of environment and the particular technology used in the agent design.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "image", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.143, 0.303, 0.854, 0.689], "content": [{"type": "image_caption", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.142, 0.269, 0.232, 0.287], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Figure 2.11"}]}, {"type": "image_body", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.143, 0.303, 0.854, 0.689], "content": ""}, {"type": "image_caption", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.142, 0.719, 0.233, 0.737], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Figure 2.12"}]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 126, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.142, 0.073, 0.857, 0.231], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "function MODEL-BASED-REFLEX-AGENT(percept) returns an action persistent: state, the agent's current conception of the world state transition_model, a description of how the next state depends on the current state and action sensor_model, a description of how the current world state is reflected in the agent's percepts rules, a set of condition-action rules action, the most recent action, initially none"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.162, 0.241, 0.83, 0.32], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "state ←UPDATE-STATE(state, action, percept, transition_model, sensor_model) rule←RULE-MATCH(state,rules) action←rule.ACTION return action"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.141, 0.327, 0.834, 0.361], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "A model-based reflex agent. It keeps track of the current state of the world, using an internal model. It then chooses an action in the same way as the reflex agent.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.391, 0.842, 0.553], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Regardless of the kind of representation used, it is seldom possible for the agent to determine the current state of a partially observable environment exactly. Instead, the box labeled “what the world is like now” (Figure 2.11 ) represents the agent’s “best guess” (or", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "sometimes best guesses, if the agent entertains multiple possibilities). For example, an automated taxi may not be able to see around the large truck that has stopped in front of it and can only guess about what may be causing the hold-up. Thus, uncertainty about the current state may be unavoidable, but the agent still has to make a decision.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.14, 0.579, 0.412, 0.602], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "2.4.4 Goal-based agents"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.139, 0.614, 0.845, 0.799], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Knowing something about the current state of the environment is not always enough to decide what to do. For example, at a road junction, the taxi can turn left, turn right, or go straight on. The correct decision depends on where the taxi is trying to get to. In other words, as well as a current state description, the agent needs some sort of goal information that describes situations that are desirable—for example, being at a particular destination. The agent program can combine this with the model (the same information as was used in the model-based reflex agent) to choose actions that achieve the goal. Figure 2.13 shows", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "the goal-based agent’s structure.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.142, 0.827, 0.233, 0.845], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Figure 2.13"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 127, "blocks": [{"type": "image", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.146, 0.075, 0.854, 0.424], "content": [{"type": "image_body", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.146, 0.075, 0.854, 0.424], "content": ""}, {"type": "image_caption", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.141, 0.435, 0.839, 0.47], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "A model-based, goal-based agent. It keeps track of the world state as well as a set of goals it is trying to achieve, and chooses an action that will (eventually) lead to the achievement of its goals."}]}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.218, 0.537, 0.259, 0.554], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Goal"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.62, 0.851, 0.736], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Sometimes goal-based action selection is straightforward—for example, when goal satisfaction results immediately from a single action. Sometimes it will be more tricky—for example, when the agent has to consider long sequences of twists and turns in order to find a way to achieve the goal. Search (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000F097.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000F097", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapters 3", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "to", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000F90E.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000F90E", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "5", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": ") and planning (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000010B83.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000010B83", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapter 11", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": ") are the", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000F097.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000F097", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000F90E.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000F90E", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000010B83.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000010B83", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "subfields of AI devoted to finding action sequences that achieve the agent’s goals.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.139, 0.762, 0.854, 0.878], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Notice that decision making of this kind is fundamentally different from the condition– action rules described earlier, in that it involves consideration of the future—both “What will happen if I do such-and-such?” and “Will that make me happy?” In the reflex agent designs, this information is not explicitly represented, because the built-in rules map directly from percepts to actions. The reflex agent brakes when it sees brake lights, period. It has no idea", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 128, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.14, 0.079, 0.852, 0.121], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "why. A goal-based agent brakes when it sees brake lights because that’s the only action that it predicts will achieve its goal of not hitting other cars.", "styles": ["bold"]}], "continues_prev": true}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.139, 0.15, 0.838, 0.287], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Although the goal-based agent appears less efficient, it is more flexible because the knowledge that supports its decisions is represented explicitly and can be modified. For example, a goal-based agent’s behavior can easily be changed to go to a different destination, simply by specifying that destination as the goal. The reflex agent’s rules for when to turn and when to go straight will work only for a single destination; they must all be replaced to go somewhere new.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.141, 0.314, 0.428, 0.336], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "2.4.5 Utility-based agents"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.349, 0.845, 0.51], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Goals alone are not enough to generate high-quality behavior in most environments. For example, many action sequences will get the taxi to its destination (thereby achieving the goal), but some are quicker, safer, more reliable, or cheaper than others. Goals just provide a crude binary distinction between “happy” and “unhappy” states. A more general performance measure should allow a comparison of different world states according to exactly how happy they would make the agent. Because “happy” does not sound very scientific, economists and computer scientists use the term utility instead.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "7", "styles": ["superscript"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.141, 0.537, 0.706, 0.552], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "7 The word “utility” here refers to “the quality of being useful,” not to the electric company or waterworks.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.218, 0.616, 0.272, 0.634], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Utility"}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.139, 0.7, 0.854, 0.837], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "We have already seen that a performance measure assigns a score to any given sequence of environment states, so it can easily distinguish between more and less desirable ways of getting to the taxi’s destination. An agent’s utility function is essentially an internalization of the performance measure. Provided that the internal utility function and the external performance measure are in agreement, an agent that chooses actions to maximize its utility will be rational according to the external performance measure."}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.219, 0.902, 0.335, 0.92], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Utility function"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 129, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.139, 0.138, 0.845, 0.348], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Let us emphasize again that this is not the only way to be rational—we have already seen a rational agent program for the vacuum world (Figure 2.8 ) that has no idea what its utility", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "function is—but, like goal-based agents, a utility-based agent has many advantages in terms of flexibility and learning. Furthermore, in two kinds of cases, goals are inadequate but a utility-based agent can still make rational decisions. First, when there are conflicting goals, only some of which can be achieved (for example, speed and safety), the utility function specifies the appropriate tradeoff. Second, when there are several goals that the agent can aim for, none of which can be achieved with certainty, utility provides a way in which the likelihood of success can be weighed against the importance of the goals.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.139, 0.375, 0.855, 0.633], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Partial observability and nondeterminism are ubiquitous in the real world, and so, therefore, is decision making under uncertainty. Technically speaking, a rational utility-based agent chooses the action that maximizes the expected utility of the action outcomes—that is, the utility the agent expects to derive, on average, given the probabilities and utilities of each outcome. (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015390.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015390", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Appendix A", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "defines expectation more precisely.) In", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000012194.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000012194", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapter 16", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": ", we show that", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015390.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015390", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000012194.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000012194", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "any rational agent must behave as if it possesses a utility function whose expected value it tries to maximize. An agent that possesses an explicit utility function can make rational decisions with a general-purpose algorithm that does not depend on the specific utility function being maximized. In this way, the “global” definition of rationality—designating as rational those agent functions that have the highest performance—is turned into a “local” constraint on rational-agent designs that can be expressed in a simple program.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.218, 0.697, 0.336, 0.715], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Expected utility", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.78, 0.837, 0.895], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The utility-based agent structure appears in Figure 2.14 . Utility-based agent programs", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "appear in", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000012194.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000012194", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapters 16", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "and", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP70010171580000000000000000124DA.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000124DA", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "17", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": ", where we design decision-making agents that must", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "handle the uncertainty inherent in nondeterministic or partially observable environments. Decision making in multiagent environments is also studied in the framework of utility theory, as explained in", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000012B87.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000012B87", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapter 18", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": ".", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000012194.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000012194", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 130, "blocks": [{"type": "image", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.145, 0.115, 0.853, 0.466], "content": [{"type": "image_caption", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.142, 0.08, 0.234, 0.098], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Figure 2.14"}]}, {"type": "image_body", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.145, 0.115, 0.853, 0.466], "content": ""}, {"type": "image_caption", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.14, 0.479, 0.819, 0.545], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "A model-based, utility-based agent. It uses a model of the world, along with a utility function that measures its preferences among states of the world. Then it chooses the action that leads to the best expected utility, where expected utility is computed by averaging over all possible outcome states, weighted by the probability of the outcome."}]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.574, 0.855, 0.833], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "At this point, the reader may be wondering, “Is it that simple? We just build agents that maximize expected utility, and we’re done?” It’s true that such agents would be intelligent, but it’s not simple. A utility-based agent has to model and keep track of its environment, tasks that have involved a great deal of research on perception, representation, reasoning, and learning. The results of this research fill many of the chapters of this book. Choosing the utility-maximizing course of action is also a difficult task, requiring ingenious algorithms that fill several more chapters. Even with these algorithms, perfect rationality is usually unachievable in practice because of computational complexity, as we noted in", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000EBA2.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000EBA2", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapter 1", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": ".", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "We also note that not all utility-based agents are model-based; we will see in", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000013FE4.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000013FE4", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapters 22", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "and", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000014C8B.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000014C8B", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "26", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "that a model-free agent can learn what action is best in a particular situation", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000EBA2.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000EBA2", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "without ever learning exactly how that action changes the environment.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.218, 0.896, 0.345, 0.914], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Model-free agent"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 131, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.139, 0.132, 0.851, 0.175], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Finally, all of this assumes that the designer can specify the utility function correctly;", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP70010171580000000000000000124DA.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000124DA", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapters 17", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": ",", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000012B87.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000012B87", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "18", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": ", and", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000013FE4.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000013FE4", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "22", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "consider the issue of unknown utility functions in more depth.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP70010171580000000000000000124DA.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000124DA", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000012B87.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000012B87", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000013FE4.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000013FE4", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.141, 0.202, 0.385, 0.224], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "2.4.6 Learning agents"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.139, 0.236, 0.855, 0.421], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "We have described agent programs with various methods for selecting actions. We have not,", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000166D4", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "so far, explained how the agent programs come into being. In his famous early paper, Turing (1950) considers the idea of actually programming his intelligent machines by hand. He", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "estimates how much work this might take and concludes, “Some more expeditious method seems desirable.” The method he proposes is to build learning machines and then to teach them. In many areas of AI, this is now the preferred method for creating state-of-the-art systems. Any type of agent (model-based, goal-based, utility-based, etc.) can be built as a learning agent (or not).", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.45, 0.849, 0.586], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Learning has another advantage, as we noted earlier: it allows the agent to operate in initially unknown environments and to become more competent than its initial knowledge alone might allow. In this section, we briefly introduce the main ideas of learning agents. Throughout the book, we comment on opportunities and methods for learning in particular kinds of agents.", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000001316E.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001316E", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapters 19", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "–", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000013FE4.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000013FE4", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "22", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "go into much more depth on the learning algorithms", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000001316E.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001316E", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000013FE4.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000013FE4", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "themselves.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.218, 0.653, 0.349, 0.67], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Learning element", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.218, 0.749, 0.374, 0.765], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Performance element", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.139, 0.833, 0.847, 0.898], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "A learning agent can be divided into four conceptual components, as shown in Figure 2.15 . The most important distinction is between the learning element, which is", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "responsible for making improvements, and the performance element, which is responsible", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 132, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.139, 0.078, 0.842, 0.17], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "for selecting external actions. The performance element is what we have previously considered to be the entire agent: it takes in percepts and decides on actions. The learning element uses feedback from the critic on how the agent is doing and determines how the performance element should be modified to do better in the future.", "styles": ["bold"]}], "continues_prev": true}, {"type": "image", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.144, 0.225, 0.854, 0.614], "content": [{"type": "image_caption", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.142, 0.198, 0.233, 0.216], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Figure 2.15"}]}, {"type": "image_body", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.144, 0.225, 0.854, 0.614], "content": ""}, {"type": "image_caption", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.141, 0.626, 0.856, 0.676], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "A general learning agent. The “performance element” box represents what we have previously considered to be the whole agent program. Now, the “learning element” box gets to modify that program to improve its performance."}]}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.218, 0.744, 0.265, 0.76], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Critic"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.139, 0.826, 0.852, 0.894], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The design of the learning element depends very much on the design of the performance element. When trying to design an agent that learns a certain capability, the first question is not “How am I going to get it to learn this?” but “What kind of performance element will my", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 133, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.139, 0.079, 0.846, 0.121], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "agent use to do this once it has learned how?” Given a design for the performance element, learning mechanisms can be constructed to improve every part of the agent.", "styles": ["bold"]}], "continues_prev": true}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.139, 0.15, 0.856, 0.312], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The critic tells the learning element how well the agent is doing with respect to a fixed performance standard. The critic is necessary because the percepts themselves provide no indication of the agent’s success. For example, a chess program could receive a percept indicating that it has checkmated its opponent, but it needs a performance standard to know that this is a good thing; the percept itself does not say so. It is important that the performance standard be fixed. Conceptually, one should think of it as being outside the agent altogether because the agent must not modify it to fit its own behavior.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.139, 0.34, 0.857, 0.573], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The last component of the learning agent is the problem generator. It is responsible for suggesting actions that will lead to new and informative experiences. If the performance element had its way, it would keep doing the actions that are best, given what it knows, but if the agent is willing to explore a little and do some perhaps suboptimal actions in the short run, it might discover much better actions for the long run. The problem generator’s job is to suggest these exploratory actions. This is what scientists do when they carry out experiments. Galileo did not think that dropping rocks from the top of a tower in Pisa was valuable in itself. He was not trying to break the rocks or to modify the brains of unfortunate pedestrians. His aim was to modify his own brain by identifying a better theory of the motion of objects.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.218, 0.638, 0.356, 0.655], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Problem generator", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.139, 0.721, 0.846, 0.907], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The learning element can make changes to any of the “knowledge” components shown in the agent diagrams (Figures 2.9, 2.11, 2.13, and 2.14). The simplest cases involve learning directly from the percept sequence. Observation of pairs of successive states of the environment can allow the agent to learn “What my actions do” and “How the world evolves” in response to its actions. For example, if the automated taxi exerts a certain braking pressure when driving on a wet road, then it will soon find out how much deceleration is actually achieved, and whether it skids off the road. The problem generator might identify certain parts of the model that are in need of improvement and suggest", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 134, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.14, 0.079, 0.792, 0.12], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "experiments, such as trying out the brakes on different road surfaces under different conditions.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.139, 0.15, 0.849, 0.264], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Improving the model components of a model-based agent so that they conform better with reality is almost always a good idea, regardless of the external performance standard. (In some cases, it is better from a computational point of view to have a simple but slightly inaccurate model rather than a perfect but fiendishly complex model.) Information from the external standard is needed when trying to learn a reflex component or a utility function.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.139, 0.293, 0.852, 0.455], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "For example, suppose the taxi-driving agent receives no tips from passengers who have been thoroughly shaken up during the trip. The external performance standard must inform the agent that the loss of tips is a negative contribution to its overall performance; then the agent might be able to learn that violent maneuvers do not contribute to its own utility. In a sense, the performance standard distinguishes part of the incoming percept as a reward (or penalty) that provides direct feedback on the quality of the agent’s behavior. Hard-wired performance standards such as pain and hunger in animals can be understood in this way.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.218, 0.52, 0.282, 0.535], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Reward", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.217, 0.616, 0.28, 0.633], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Penalty"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.139, 0.699, 0.854, 0.836], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "More generally, human choices can provide information about human preferences. For example, suppose the taxi does not know that people generally don’t like loud noises, and settles on the idea of blowing its horn continuously as a way of ensuring that pedestrians know it’s coming. The consequent human behavior—covering ears, using bad language, and possibly cutting the wires to the horn—would provide evidence to the agent with which to update its utility function. This issue is discussed further in", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000013FE4.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000013FE4", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapter 22", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": ".", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000013FE4.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000013FE4", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.139, 0.865, 0.829, 0.908], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "In summary, agents have a variety of components, and those components can be represented in many ways within the agent program, so there appears to be great variety", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 135, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.139, 0.079, 0.856, 0.17], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "among learning methods. There is, however, a single unifying theme. Learning in intelligent agents can be summarized as a process of modification of each component of the agent to bring the components into closer agreement with the available feedback information, thereby improving the overall performance of the agent.", "styles": ["bold"]}], "continues_prev": true}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.14, 0.195, 0.717, 0.218], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "2.4.7 How the components of agent programs work"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.139, 0.23, 0.858, 0.391], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "We have described agent programs (in very high-level terms) as consisting of various components, whose function it is to answer questions such as: “What is the world like now?” “What action should I do now?” “What do my actions do?” The next question for a student of AI is, “How on Earth do these components work?” It takes about a thousand pages to begin to answer that question properly, but here we want to draw the reader’s attention to some basic distinctions among the various ways that the components can represent the environment that the agent inhabits.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.42, 0.85, 0.558], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Roughly speaking, we can place the representations along an axis of increasing complexity and expressive power—atomic, factored, and structured. To illustrate these ideas, it helps to consider a particular agent component, such as the one that deals with “What my actions do.” This component describes the changes that might occur in the environment as the result of taking an action, and Figure 2.16 provides schematic depictions of how those", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "transitions might be represented.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "image", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.144, 0.703, 0.277, 0.744], "content": [{"type": "image_caption", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.141, 0.586, 0.234, 0.604], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Figure 2.16"}]}, {"type": "image_body", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.144, 0.703, 0.277, 0.744], "content": ""}, {"type": "image_caption", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.163, 0.807, 0.252, 0.824], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "(a) Atomic"}]}]}, {"type": "image", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.33, 0.658, 0.555, 0.791], "content": [{"type": "image_body", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.33, 0.658, 0.555, 0.791], "content": ""}, {"type": "image_caption", "index": 8, "bbox": [0.394, 0.806, 0.494, 0.824], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "(b) Factored"}]}]}, {"type": "image", "index": 9, "bbox": [0.606, 0.66, 0.855, 0.788], "content": [{"type": "image_body", "index": 9, "bbox": [0.606, 0.66, 0.855, 0.788], "content": ""}, {"type": "image_caption", "index": 10, "bbox": [0.679, 0.806, 0.792, 0.824], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "(c) Structured"}]}, {"type": "image_caption", "index": 11, "bbox": [0.139, 0.835, 0.855, 0.915], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Three ways to represent states and the transitions between them. (a) Atomic representation: a state (such as B or C) is a black box with no internal structure; (b) Factored representation: a state consists of a vector of attribute values; values can be Boolean, real-valued, or one of a fixed set of symbols. (c) Structured representation: a state includes objects, each of which may have attributes of its own as well as relationships to other objects."}]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 136, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.14, 0.08, 0.856, 0.288], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "In an atomic representation each state of the world is indivisible—it has no internal structure. Consider the task of finding a driving route from one end of a country to the other via some sequence of cities (we address this problem in", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000F0A7.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000F0C0", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Figure 3.1", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "on page", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000F0A7.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000F0B1", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "64", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "). For the", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "purposes of solving this problem, it may suffice to reduce the state of the world to just the name of the city we are in—a single atom of knowledge, a “black box” whose only discernible property is that of being identical to or different from another black box. The standard algorithms underlying search and game-playing (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000F097.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000F097", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapters 3", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "–", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000F90E.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000F90E", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "5", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "), hidden", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Markov models (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000011529.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000011529", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapter 14", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "), and Markov decision processes (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP70010171580000000000000000124DA.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000124DA", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapter 17", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": ") all work with", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000F0A7.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000F0C0", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000F097.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000F097", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "atomic representations.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.217, 0.353, 0.383, 0.37], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Atomic representation", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.14, 0.436, 0.851, 0.74], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "A factored representation splits up each state into a fixed set of variables or attributes, each of which can have a value. Consider a higher-fidelity description for the same driving problem, where we need to be concerned with more than just atomic location in one city or another; we might need to pay attention to how much gas is in the tank, our current GPS coordinates, whether or not the oil warning light is working, how much money we have for tolls, what station is on the radio, and so on. While two different atomic states have nothing in common—they are just different black boxes—two different factored states can share some attributes (such as being at some particular GPS location) and not others (such as having lots of gas or having no gas); this makes it much easier to work out how to turn one state into another. Many important areas of AI are based on factored representations, including constraint satisfaction algorithms (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000FBA5.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000FBA5", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapter 6", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "), propositional logic (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000FE31.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000FE31", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapter 7", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "),", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "planning (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000010B83.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000010B83", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapter 11", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "), Bayesian networks (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000010E81.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000010E81", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapters 12", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "–", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000012194.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000012194", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "16", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "), and various machine", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000FBA5.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000FBA5", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000FE31.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000FE31", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000010B83.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000010B83", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "learning algorithms.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.218, 0.805, 0.392, 0.822], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Factored representation", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.219, 0.901, 0.286, 0.917], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Variable"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 137, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.217, 0.15, 0.291, 0.168], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Attribute", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.219, 0.247, 0.268, 0.264], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Value"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.14, 0.329, 0.856, 0.589], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "For many purposes, we need to understand the world as having things in it that are related to each other, not just variables with values. For example, we might notice that a large truck ahead of us is reversing into the driveway of a dairy farm, but a loose cow is blocking the truck’s path. A factored representation is unlikely to be pre-equipped with the attribute TruckAheadBackingIntoDairyFarmDrivewayBlockedByLooseCow with value true or false. Instead, we would need a structured representation, in which objects such as cows and trucks and their various and varying relationships can be described explicitly (see Figure 2.16(c) ). Structured representations underlie relational databases and first-order logic", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "(", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000001039E.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001039E", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapters 8", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ",", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000001063E.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001063E", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "9", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ", and", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000001094A.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001094A", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "10", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "), first-order probability models (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000011E2C.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000011E2C", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapter 15", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "), and much of natural language understanding (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000001427E.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001427E", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapters 23", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "and", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000001461E.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001461E", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "24", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "). In fact, much of what humans", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000001427E.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001427E", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000001461E.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001461E", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "express in natural language concerns objects and their relationships.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.218, 0.652, 0.408, 0.67], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Structured representation"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.139, 0.735, 0.858, 0.921], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "As we mentioned earlier, the axis along which atomic, factored, and structured representations lie is the axis of increasing expressiveness. Roughly speaking, a more expressive representation can capture, at least as concisely, everything a less expressive one can capture, plus some more. Often, the more expressive language is much more concise; for example, the rules of chess can be written in a page or two of a structured-representation language such as first-order logic but require thousands of pages when written in a factoredrepresentation language such as propositional logic and around pages when written in", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "10 "}, {"type": "text", "content": "an atomic language such as that of finite-state automata. On the other hand, reasoning and", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 138, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.14, 0.079, 0.846, 0.146], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "learning become more complex as the expressive power of the representation increases. To gain the benefits of expressive representations while avoiding their drawbacks, intelligent systems for the real world may need to operate at all points along the axis simultaneously.", "styles": ["bold"]}], "continues_prev": true}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.218, 0.21, 0.331, 0.229], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Expressiveness", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.218, 0.307, 0.387, 0.325], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Localist representation", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.39, 0.855, 0.649], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Another axis for representation involves the mapping of concepts to locations in physical memory, whether in a computer or in a brain. If there is a one-to-one mapping between concepts and memory locations, we call that a localist representation. On the other hand, if the representation of a concept is spread over many memory locations, and each memory location is employed as part of the representation of multiple different concepts, we call that a distributed representation. Distributed representations are more robust against noise and information loss. With a localist representation, the mapping from concept to memory location is arbitrary, and if a transmission error garbles a few bits, we might confuse Truck with the unrelated concept Truce. But with a distributed representation, you can think of each concept representing a point in multidimensional space, and if you garble a few bits you move to a nearby point in that space, which will have similar meaning.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "footer", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.218, 0.712, 0.413, 0.73], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Distributed representation"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 139, "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.141, 0.127, 0.278, 0.152], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Summary"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.139, 0.166, 0.843, 0.209], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "This chapter has been something of a whirlwind tour of AI, which we have conceived of as the science of agent design. The major points to recall are as follows:", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.154, 0.238, 0.855, 0.28], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "An agent is something that perceives and acts in an environment. The agent function for an agent specifies the action taken by the agent in response to any percept sequence.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.154, 0.285, 0.838, 0.351], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The performance measure evaluates the behavior of the agent in an environment. A rational agent acts so as to maximize the expected value of the performance measure, given the percept sequence it has seen so far.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.154, 0.356, 0.838, 0.422], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "A task environment specification includes the performance measure, the external environment, the actuators, and the sensors. In designing an agent, the first step must always be to specify the task environment as fully as possible.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.154, 0.428, 0.811, 0.515], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Task environments vary along several significant dimensions. They can be fully or partially observable, single-agent or multiagent, deterministic or nondeterministic, episodic or sequential, static or dynamic, discrete or continuous, and known or unknown.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.154, 0.522, 0.847, 0.588], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "In cases where the performance measure is unknown or hard to specify correctly, there is a significant risk of the agent optimizing the wrong objective. In such cases the agent design should reflect uncertainty about the true objective.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.154, 0.594, 0.852, 0.683], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The agent program implements the agent function. There exists a variety of basic agent program designs reflecting the kind of information made explicit and used in the decision process. The designs vary in efficiency, compactness, and flexibility. The appropriate design of the agent program depends on the nature of the environment.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 8, "bbox": [0.154, 0.688, 0.846, 0.777], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Simple reflex agents respond directly to percepts, whereas model-based reflex agents maintain internal state to track aspects of the world that are not evident in the current percept. Goal-based agents act to achieve their goals, and utility-based agents try to maximize their own expected “happiness.”", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 9, "bbox": [0.154, 0.783, 0.639, 0.801], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "All agents can improve their performance through learning.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 140, "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.141, 0.125, 0.633, 0.152], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Bibliographical and Historical Notes"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.139, 0.166, 0.854, 0.4], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The central role of action in intelligence—the notion of practical reasoning—goes back at least as far as Aristotle’s Nicomachean Ethics. Practical reasoning was also the subject of McCarthy’s influential paper", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000016136", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "“Programs with Common Sense” (1958)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ". The fields of robotics and control theory are, by their very nature, concerned principally with physical agents. The concept of a controller in control theory is identical to that of an agent in AI. Perhaps surprisingly, AI has concentrated for most of its history on isolated components of agents— question-answering systems, theorem-provers, vision systems, and so on—rather than on whole agents. The discussion of agents in the text by", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015BB5", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Genesereth and Nilsson (1987)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "was an influential exception. The whole-agent view is now widely accepted and is a central theme in recent texts (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000162BF", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Padgham and Winikoff, 2004", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ";", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015E2D", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Jones, 2007", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ";", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000016372", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Poole and Mackworth, 2017", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ").", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.217, 0.464, 0.3, 0.481], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Controller"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.547, 0.854, 0.663], "content": [{"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000EBA2.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000EBA2", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapter 1", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "traced the roots of the concept of rationality in philosophy and economics. In", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "AI, the concept was of peripheral interest until the mid-1980s, when it began to suffuse", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000016372", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "many discussions about the proper technical foundations of the field. A paper by Jon Doyle (1983) predicted that rational agent design would come to be seen as the core mission of A", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "I, while other popular topics would spin off to form new disciplines.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.139, 0.689, 0.851, 0.9], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Careful attention to the properties of the environment and their consequences for rational agent design is most apparent in the control theory tradition—for example, classical control systems (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015A1C", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Dorf and Bishop, 2004", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ";", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015ED6", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Kirk, 2004", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ") handle fully observable, deterministic", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000156AE", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "environments; stochastic optimal control (", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "Kumar and Varaiya, 1986", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000156AE", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "; Bertsekas and Shreve,", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "2007) handles partially observable, stochastic environments; and hybrid control (Henzinge", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015D19", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "r and Sastry, 1998;", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Cassandras and Lygeros, 2006", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015D19", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ") deals with environments containing both", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "discrete and continuous elements. The distinction between fully and partially observable environments is also central in the dynamic programming literature developed in the field of operations research (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000016394", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Puterman, 1994", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "), which we discuss in", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP70010171580000000000000000124DA.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000124DA", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapter 17", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": ".", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP70010171580000000000000000124DA.xhtml#P70010171580000000000000000124DA", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}]}]}]}, {"page_idx": 141, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.139, 0.078, 0.856, 0.194], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Although simple reflex agents were central to behaviorist psychology (see", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP700101715800000000000000000EBA2.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000000EBA2", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapter 1", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "),", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "most AI researchers view them as too simple to provide much leverage. (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001644C", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Rosenschein (1985)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "and", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001578A", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Brooks (1986)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "questioned this assumption; see", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000014C8B.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000014C8B", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Chapter 26", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": ".) A great deal of work has", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001561B", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "gone into finding efficient algorithms for keeping track of complex environments (Bar-Shalom et al., 2001;", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Choset et al., 2005", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001561B", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ";", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Simon, 2006", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001561B", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "), most of it in the probabilistic sett", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "ing.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.139, 0.221, 0.849, 0.598], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Goal-based agents are presupposed in everything from Aristotle’s view of practical", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015AEC", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "reasoning to McCarthy’s early papers on logical AI. Shakey the Robot (Fikes and Nilsson, 1971;", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Nilsson, 1984", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015AEC", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": ") was the first robotic embodiment of a logical, goal-based agent. A fu", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "ll logical analysis of goal-based agents appeared in", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015BB5", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Genesereth and Nilsson (1987)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ", and a goal-based programming methodology called agent-oriented programming was developed by", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000016561", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Shoham (1993)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ". The agent-based approach is now extremely popular in software engineering (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001588E", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Ciancarini and Wooldridge, 2001", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "). It has also infiltrated the area of operating systems, where autonomic computing refers to computer systems and networks that monitor and control themselves with a perceive–act loop and machine learning methods (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015EBE", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Kephart and Chess, 2003", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "). Noting that a collection of agent programs designed to work well together in a true multiagent environment necessarily exhibits modularity—the programs share no internal state and communicate with each other only through the environment—it is common within the field of multiagent systems to design the agent program of a single agent as a collection of autonomous sub-agents. In some cases, one can even prove that the resulting system gives the same optimal solutions as a monolithic design.", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.217, 0.662, 0.384, 0.679], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Autonomic computing"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.139, 0.745, 0.852, 0.859], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The goal-based view of agents also dominates the cognitive psychology tradition in the area of problem solving, beginning with the enormously influential Human Problem Solving (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000016252", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Newell and Simon, 1972", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ") and running through all of Newell’s later work (", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P700101715800000000000000001624A", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Newell, 1990", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": "). Goals, further analyzed as desires (general) and intentions (currently pursued), are central to the influential theory of agents developed by", "styles": ["bold"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " "}, {"type": "hyperlink", "url": "https://jigsaw.vitalsource.com/books/9780134671932/epub/OPS/xhtml/fileP7001017158000000000000000015495.xhtml#P7001017158000000000000000015766", "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Michael Bratman (1987)", "styles": ["bold"]}]}, {"type": "text", "content": ".", "styles": ["bold"]}]}]}], "is_full_document": false, "schema": "docvortex.middle", "schema_version": "2.0"}